<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/foundational/design-is-deciding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Design is not decoration — it is <strong>deciding on the user's behalf</strong>: what to see
    first, what happens when you tap, what happens when you're wrong. The craft is making hundreds of
    small decisions so coherently that the interface — or the chair, the kettle, the bus map — feels
    obvious before you think. Three decisions dominate: <strong>hierarchy</strong> (what matters
    most), <strong>affordance</strong> (what looks usable) and <strong>feedback</strong> (confirming
    every action instantly). But those interface-level decisions rest on a deeper layer the syllabus
    names explicitly: every design is an answer to a chain of influences — who the user is, where the
    product will be used, the sizes and abilities of real bodies, what looks right in this culture,
    what the design's life does to the planet, and what its existence does to the market and society
    around it. This lesson walks that whole chain, from thumb-reach physics to global waste.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Fitts's law (1954) — bigger, closer targets are faster to hit; drag the width and the maths agrees" widget="plotter" config={fittsCfg} />

  <p>
    Fitts's law quantifies a truth every thumb already knows: hitting a target takes time proportional
    to the logarithm of distance-over-size (the "index of difficulty"). It is why phone buttons live
    at the bottom of the screen (closest to your thumb's arc), why the close button on a modal is
    smaller than the primary action (you are steering the user's choice), and why tiny tap targets
    are design failures with a formula behind them. Note the logarithm's shape in the plot: doubling
    a button's width matters enormously when it is tiny and almost not at all when it is already
    large — the first 40 pixels are worth more than the next 160. Design is full of returns like
    this, and knowing where the curve flattens is knowing where to stop spending.
  </p>

  <h2>User needs and the context of use</h2>
  <p>
    Everything starts with a disciplined answer to: <em>who needs this, to do what, in what
    situation?</em> "User needs" is not a demographic sketch — it is a job under conditions. The
    same map app is a different product for a pedestrian in Tokyo rain, a courier on a bike with
    gloves on, and a driver at night; each context changes the priorities (glanceability, one-handed
    reach, glare, noise). The professional tools are <strong>personas</strong> (concrete archetypes
    built from research, so the team argues about "Amira, 34, commutes on a crowded train" rather
    than "the user"), <strong>jobs-to-be-done</strong> framing (people "hire" a product to do a job —
    the drill bought for the hole), and <strong>context-of-use analysis</strong>: environment
    (lighting, noise, weather, stress), frequency of use (an interface used hourly must be fast and
    muscle-memorised; one used yearly must be self-explanatory — different designs entirely), and
    consequences of error (a music app's undo versus a medical pump's dosing error are held to
    different standards of certainty). A design decision made without its context is the classic
    failure mode: the beautiful interface that assumes silence, two free hands and perfect
    connectivity.
  </p>

  <h2>Anthropometrics and ergonomics: designing for real bodies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anthropometrics</strong> is the measurement of human bodies, and its central lesson is
    the distribution: people vary enormously in height, reach, grip strength and visual acuity, so
    the designer must ask <em>which percentile</em> each dimension is being set for. The standard
    rule — design for the <strong>5th to 95th percentile</strong>, never the average — follows from
    a fact worth internalising: the "average person" exists on almost no dimension at once, so a
    cockpit designed for the average of five measurements would fit approximately nobody (the
    conclusion Gilbert Daniels reached measuring 4,000+ pilots in 1950, grounding the modern
    adjustable cockpit). The practical moves: <em>clearance</em> dimensions (doorways, seat width)
    are sized to the large percentile; <em>reach</em> dimensions (controls, shelves) to the small;
    and where the population's range is wide, <strong>adjustability</strong> replaces a fixed choice
    — the office chair, the car seat, the standing desk. <strong>Ergonomics</strong> (human factors)
    generalises this from size to fit between person and product: cognitive load, posture over time,
    force required, and the feedback loop between user and tool. <strong>Inclusivity</strong> is the
    ethical and commercial extension: roughly one person in six lives with significant disability,
    and the classic finding is that designing for the edge case improves the product for everyone —
    curb cuts serve wheelchair users and parents with prams and travellers with suitcases; captions
    serve deaf audiences and everyone on a loud train. And <strong>safety</strong> folds in here:
    guard the hazard, fail visibly rather than silently (Norman's interlocks and confirmations),
    and design for the error you did not want but know is coming.
  </p>

  <h2>Aesthetics, affordances and the psychology of obvious</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Aesthetics</strong> in design is not garnish — it does cognitive work. The
    <em>aesthetic–usability effect</em> (Kurosu and Kashimura, 1995) found people rate attractive
    interfaces as easier to use, and tolerate their flaws longer; beauty buys patience. But the
    deeper layer is <strong>affordance</strong>, Don Norman's load-bearing concept (from
    psychologist J. J. Gibson): an affordance is the relationship between an object and a user that
    makes an action possible — a flat plate affords pushing, a handle affords pulling. Norman's
    refinement matters for design practice: physical affordances must be paired with
    <strong>signifiers</strong> — visible markers that communicate the affordance (the painted
    "PUSH" bar) — because an affordance nobody can perceive is, functionally, absent. The doors we
    all push that say PULL are <em>Norman doors</em>, design failures so common he named them. Two
    more of Norman's concepts complete the psychology: <strong>mapping</strong> (controls should
    correspond spatially to their effects — stove knobs laid out like the burners they fire) and
    <strong>constraints</strong> (making wrong actions impossible or unlikely — the USB-C plug that
    cannot be inserted wrong, the form that refuses invalid input politely). Hierarchy, then, is
    applied perception: <strong>contrast is the lever</strong> — size, weight, colour and whitespace
    all shout; <strong>whitespace is structure</strong>, not emptiness — it groups related things
    and separates unrelated ones faster than borders ever will (the Gestalt law of proximity doing
    layout work); and a design that shouts everything is whispering.
  </p>

  <h2>Sustainability: the six Rs</h2>
  <p>
    Design's decisions extend through the product's whole life, and the syllabus's framework is the
    hierarchy of <strong>Rethink, Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair, Recycle</strong> — ordered
    deliberately from most to least effective. <strong>Rethink</strong>: question the need — does
    the product need to exist in this form at all (a library of tools versus everyone owning a
    drill)? <strong>Refuse</strong>: design out the harmful part — no plastic film on a cucumber,
    no charger bundled with every device. <strong>Reduce</strong>: less material, lighter
    packaging, concentrated refills — the smallest change with the biggest volume effect.
    <strong>Reuse</strong>: build for a second life — refillable bottles, modular phones.
    <strong>Repair</strong>: the most design-active R, because repairability is decided at the
    drawing stage — screws instead of glue, standard parts, published manuals; the "right to
    repair" movement is design policy catching up with design decisions. <strong>Recycle</strong>
    comes last, honestly: it is the weakest R (most recycling is downcycling, and collection rates
    for plastics are dismal) — a useful reminder that the hierarchy exists because designers
    instinctively reach for the last R when the first one was the real answer. The counter-example
    that motivates the whole section is <strong>planned obsolescence</strong> — from the 1924 Phoebus
    light-bulb cartel (agreeing to shorten bulb life) to software that slows older hardware —
    design's shadow side, where the same skill that extends a product's useful life is used to
    curtail it.
  </p>

  <h2>Existing designs, trends, culture — and design's impact</h2>
  <p>
    No designer starts from zero. <strong>Existing designs</strong> are the raw material:
    benchmarking the category (what do users already expect — the muscle memory of the back button),
    copying structure where convention serves users and breaking it only where breaking is
    justified. <strong>Fashion and trends</strong> move faster than ergonomics and must be handled
    accordingly — skeuomorphism gave way to flat design gave way to soft gradients, mostly as
    taste cycles, while the underlying human factors stayed constant; the discipline is knowing
    which layer a trend lives in. <strong>Society and different cultures</strong> enter everywhere:
    colour symbolism varies (white for weddings here, mourning there), reading direction flips
    layout logic (right-to-left scripts mirror navigation), icon conventions fail across borders
    (the mailbox, the check mark), and the highest-performing designs are culturally adapted, not
    just translated. And <strong>design's impact on society and markets</strong> is the reflexive
    question the syllabus insists on: design creates markets (the smartphone was a designed
    convergence that made app economies possible), shapes behaviour (seatbelt chimes, default
    options — defaults are the most powerful "decision" a designer makes, per the behavioural
    economics literature on organ-donation consent rates), and can weaponise the same psychology —
    <strong>dark patterns</strong> (a term coined by Harry Brignull, 2010): confirm-shaming,
    hidden costs at checkout, roach motels that are easy to enter and designed to be hard to leave.
    The same Fitts's-law expertise that places the primary action under your thumb can place the
    "cancel subscription" link where your thumb never goes. Ethics is not adjacent to this
    subject; it is the same lever, aimed.
  </p>

  <h2>Never let a click land in silence: feedback engineering</h2>
  <p>
    The rules above converge on a feedback habit worth its own section, because it is where
    hierarchy and affordance meet time. Every user action needs a <em>visible result within roughly
    100 milliseconds</em> — the perception threshold — or the system feels broken: buttons show a
    pressed state, forms disable their submit control while working, uploads show progress, and
    failures explain themselves. The subtlety is honest feedback: <em>optimistic UI</em> (showing
    the sent message instantly, rolling back on failure) trades truth for speed and must be
    engineered carefully — a message that quietly fails after appearing to send is worse than a
    visible spinner. Loading states are themselves decisions: a bare spinner over one second teaches
    users to distrust the system; a skeleton layout teaches them what is coming. And
    <strong>error messages are design</strong>, often the most-seen screen in a product: say what
    happened, why, and what to do next — "Card declined — try another card or contact your bank",
    not "Error 402". One more hierarchy rule follows from all of it: <strong>one primary action per
    screen</strong> — if two buttons look equally important, the design has decided nothing. The
    test of feedback is brutal and cheap: use the product with sound off, from across the room, and
    ask whether the screen's state tells the story by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative layer, for the committed: <strong>Hick's law</strong> — reaction time grows with
    the logarithm of the number of choices, which is why menus shave options and why grouping
    choices into categories works (it lowers effective choice count per decision); with Fitts's law
    it predicts interaction time almost mechanically, and together they justify the field's deepest
    interface habits: few, large, well-spaced targets. <strong>Feedback timing has thresholds:</strong>
    the classic numbers — perception of immediate response under ~100 ms, a felt pause by ~1 s,
    abandonment risk past ~10 s — set the budget for loading states, optimistic UI and progress
    bars: <strong>never let a click land in silence</strong>, because silent interfaces get retried,
    usually destructively. <strong>And validation remains the field's honesty device:</strong>
    usability tests (the long-standing Nielsen–Landauer finding that around five users surface the
    large majority of severe usability problems — because usability problems are shared, not
    individually unique), analytics for what people <em>do</em>, interviews for why.
    <strong>Taste gets you to a candidate; evidence decides.</strong> The best designers are the
    ones most willing to be wrong early, on paper, where being wrong is free — sketches, prototypes,
    and the humility that the user's mental model, not the designer's, is the one the product has to
    live inside. Design is deciding; the discipline is deciding <em>well</em>, for bodies and
    contexts and cultures you did not come from, and being able to say why each decision was made —
    with a formula, an observation, or a percentile — when someone asks.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fitts (1954); Hick (1952); Norman, The Design of Everyday Things (1988); Nielsen & Landauer (1993); Kurosu & Kashimura (1995); Brignull on dark patterns (2010); UK Design & Technology syllabus treatment of the six Rs.</p>
</TopicPage>
